theory CompliantCeremony
begin

/*
================================================================================
  WITNESS NOMINATION -- what an honest participant refuses to sign
================================================================================

  WHICH TREE THIS IS.  `compliant/`.  The obligation modelled here is one the
  design says in terms cannot be checked by anyone else, and names the party
  who can:

    `light-client-requirements.md` -- "Refuse to sign a record attributing to
      you a witness you did not nominate.  Your client knows exactly which
      witnesses it nominated; an entry claiming `nominated_by = you` outside
      that set is the counterparty dressing its own curation in your name, and
      it defeats the split check from inside an apparently balanced record.
      THE WIRE CANNOT CHECK THIS (`nominated_by` is a recorded claim) -- YOU
      CAN, AND YOU ARE THE ONLY PARTY [who can]."

  So `wire-only/ceremony.spthy` structurally cannot hold this property, and
  not through any shortcoming of that theory: `nominated_by` is a claim in a
  record, and a well-formed record can carry a false one.  The check lives in
  one participant's own memory of what it nominated.  This theory is that
  memory made into a state.

  WHAT IT DOES NOT TOUCH.  Co-presence.  That is an axiom in the wire theory
  and remains one here; nothing about nomination bears on whether anybody was
  in a room.  This theory takes the ceremony as happening and asks only whether
  the roster an honest participant signs can misattribute its own nominees.
*/

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  NEIGHBOURHOODS AND NOMINATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  design 7.1's rule is directional: you nominate from the OTHER party's
  neighbourhood.  A witness drawn from your own is the failure mode the split
  exists to prevent, so `Nominate` requires membership of the counterparty's
  neighbourhood and non-membership of the nominator's.  Overlap is possible in
  the real graph -- horizons intersect -- and the rule resolves it the strict
  way: "never from your own" is not weakened by the node also being in theirs.
*/

rule Neighbour:
    [ ]
  --[ IsNeighbour($X, $W) ]->
    [ !InNeighbourhood($X, $W) ]

rule Nominate:
    [ !InNeighbourhood($Other, $W) ]
  --[ Nominated($P, $Other, $W)
    , NotInOwn($P, $W) ]->
    [ !Nomination($P, $Other, $W) ]

restriction NeverFromYourOwnNeighbourhood:
  "All P W #i. NotInOwn(P, W) @ #i ==> not(Ex #j. IsNeighbour(P, W) @ #j)"

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE RECORD, AND THE SIGNATURE DECISION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A proposed record attributes each witness to a nominator.  The attribution
  is a CLAIM -- `In(...)`, straight off the network, chosen by whoever
  assembled the record.  That is faithful: the counterparty assembles a body
  and offers it, and the wire has no way to tell a true `nominated_by` from a
  false one.

  The honest participant's rule is the guard on `Sign`.  It signs only if its
  own memory of what it nominated agrees with what the record says it
  nominated.  `!Nomination` is persistent because a client's record of its own
  nominations is not consumed by consulting it.
*/

rule Propose_Record:
    [ Fr(~rec), In(<$W, $P, $Other>) ]
  --[ Proposed(~rec, $W, $P, $Other) ]->
    [ !Record(~rec, $W, $P, $Other) ]

/*  THE OBLIGATION AS A GUARD.  Signing consumes nothing and asserts one
    thing: a nomination of $W by $P really happened.  A record attributing a
    witness to $P that $P never nominated cannot reach this rule.          */
rule Sign_Record:
    [ !Record(rec, $W, $P, $Other), !Nomination($P, $Other, $W) ]
  --[ Signed(rec, $P), SignedAttributing(rec, $P, $W) ]->
    [ Out(<'signed', rec, $P>) ]

/*  The dishonest alternative, present so the property is not true merely
    because no rule could ever misattribute.  A client that skips the check
    signs whatever it is handed -- and this is what the wire permits, since
    such a record is well formed.  The lemmas below carve it out by name,
    which is the honest way to state a compliance property: it holds of a
    node that follows the rule, and says nothing about one that does not.  */
rule Sign_Without_Checking:
    [ !Record(rec, $W, $P, $Other) ]
  --[ Signed(rec, $P), SignedAttributing(rec, $P, $W)
    , SkippedTheCheck($P) ]->
    [ Out(<'signed', rec, $P>) ]

/*
================================================================================
  PROPERTIES
================================================================================
*/

/*  THE OBLIGATION.  A participant that ran the check signs no record
    attributing to it a witness it did not nominate.  The carve-out names the
    non-compliant rule rather than a compromise: this is a theory about
    keeping a promise, and the way to break it is to not keep it.          */
lemma no_signature_over_a_misattributed_nominee:
  all-traces
  "All rec P W #i.
      SignedAttributing(rec, P, W) @ #i
      & not(Ex #s. SkippedTheCheck(P) @ #s)
        ==> Ex Other #n. Nominated(P, Other, W) @ #n & #n < #i"

/*  THE DIRECTIONAL RULE.  No witness a compliant participant nominated is
    drawn from its own neighbourhood -- design 7.1's "never from your own",
    which is what makes the split mean anything.                           */
lemma no_nominee_from_the_nominators_own_neighbourhood:
  all-traces
  "All P Other W #i #j.
      Nominated(P, Other, W) @ #i & IsNeighbour(P, W) @ #j
        ==> F"

/*  ANTI-VACUITY.  An honest participant signs a record.                   */
lemma an_honest_signature_is_reachable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex rec P W #i.
      SignedAttributing(rec, P, W) @ #i
    & not(Ex #s. SkippedTheCheck(P) @ #s)"

/*  THE WITNESS THAT THE CHECK IS LOAD-BEARING.  A misattributed record CAN
    be signed -- by a client that skipped the check.  If this ever falsifies,
    the model has made the wire enforce something the design says it cannot,
    and the first lemma would be true for the wrong reason.                */
lemma misattribution_is_possible_without_the_check:
  exists-trace
  "Ex rec P W #i.
      SignedAttributing(rec, P, W) @ #i
    & SkippedTheCheck(P) @ #i
    & not(Ex Other #n. Nominated(P, Other, W) @ #n)"


/*
================================================================================
  CO-PRESENCE -- what a conforming participant will not sign
================================================================================

  WHY THIS IS HERE AND NOT IN `wire-only/`.  These four properties used to
  live in `wire-only/ceremony.spthy`, and a review was right that they did not
  belong: they hold only of participants who follow the signing discipline,
  and the wire cannot check that anyone did.  design 7 is explicit --
  "bilateral collusion is unpreventable" -- and design 15.4 that presence "is
  not an unforgeable primitive, and bilateral collusion defeats it
  regardless".

  The tell was that the old model let a `CoPresent` token gate the USE OF A
  PARTY'S OWN KEY.  Nothing in the protocol does that, and nothing could.
  Add a rule letting a legitimate principal sign a body it never met for --
  no compromise, its own key -- and all four lemmas falsify at once, which is
  how the partition was settled rather than argued.

  KEY COMPROMISE AND DISHONESTY ARE DIFFERENT CAPABILITIES, and conflating
  them was the actual error.  `Compromised(P)` means someone stole P's key.
  It cannot stand for "P used P's own key contrary to client policy" -- that
  needs no theft, and carving it out under the same name made the theorem
  look stronger than it was.  Here the carve-out is `SkippedTheMeeting`,
  named for what it is.
*/

rule Party:
    [ ] --[ IsParty($P) ]-> [ !Party($P) ]

rule Meeting:
    [ Fr(~cid) ]
  --[ MeetHappened($P1, $P2, ~cid) ]->
    [ CoPresent($P1, ~cid), CoPresent($P2, ~cid), !Roster($P1, $P2, ~cid) ]

/*  THE DISCIPLINE.  A conforming participant signs a presence body only for
    a meeting it was at: the token is consumed, so one meeting licenses one
    signature.                                                              */
rule Sign_Presence_Compliant:
    [ CoPresent($P, cid), !Roster($P1, $P2, cid) ]
  --[ PresenceSigned($P, $P1, $P2, cid) ]->
    [ Sig($P, $P1, $P2, cid) ]

/*  AND THE PARTY THAT DOES NOT KEEP IT.  Its own key, a body off the
    network, no meeting.  This is not an attack on the protocol -- it is two
    people deciding to lie, and the design prices it as attack cost rather
    than preventing it.                                                     */
rule Sign_Presence_Without_Meeting:
    [ !Party($P), In(<$P1, $P2, cid>) ]
  --[ PresenceSigned($P, $P1, $P2, cid), SkippedTheMeeting($P) ]->
    [ Sig($P, $P1, $P2, cid) ]

rule Accept_Presence:
    [ Sig($P1, $P1, $P2, cid), Sig($P2, $P1, $P2, cid) ]
  --[ PresenceAccepted($P1, $P2, cid) ]->
    [ ]

/*  THE CONFORMANCE THEOREM.  Among participants who kept the discipline, an
    accepted record implies the meeting happened.  This is the honest form of
    what `presence_requires_copresence` was reaching for.                   */
lemma a_record_between_conforming_participants_implies_a_meeting:
  all-traces
  "All P1 P2 cid #i.
      PresenceAccepted(P1, P2, cid) @ #i
      & not(Ex P #s. SkippedTheMeeting(P) @ #s)
        ==> Ex #m. MeetHappened(P1, P2, cid) @ #m & #m < #i"

/*  ONE MEETING, ONE ROSTER: a conforming participant's signature cannot be
    transplanted onto a different pairing, because the token it consumed
    belonged to that roster.  The honest form of
    `copresence_binds_one_roster`.                                          */
lemma a_conforming_signature_binds_one_roster:
  all-traces
  "All P1 P2 cid Q1 Q2 #i #j.
      PresenceAccepted(P1, P2, cid) @ #i & PresenceAccepted(Q1, Q2, cid) @ #j
      & not(Ex P #s. SkippedTheMeeting(P) @ #s)
        ==> P1 = Q1 & P2 = Q2"

/*  ANTI-VACUITY: conforming participants do produce records.               */
lemma a_conforming_record_is_reachable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex P1 P2 cid #i.
      PresenceAccepted(P1, P2, cid) @ #i
    & not(Ex P #s. SkippedTheMeeting(P) @ #s)"

/*  THE RESIDUAL, KEPT REACHABLE.  Two parties who simply lie produce an
    accepted record with no meeting behind it, using nothing but their own
    keys.  design 7 says this is unpreventable, so a model in which it became
    unreachable would be claiming more than the design does -- and would put
    these lemmas back in the wrong tree.                                    */
lemma collusion_produces_a_record_without_a_meeting:
  exists-trace
  "Ex P1 P2 cid #i.
      PresenceAccepted(P1, P2, cid) @ #i
    & not(Ex #m. MeetHappened(P1, P2, cid) @ #m)"


end
